{"id":"h103","run":2,"role":"test_wrong","category":"science-physics","question":"A 20 gallon automobile gasoline tank is filled exactly to the top at 0°F just before the automobile is parked in a garage where the temperature is maintained at 70°F. How much gasoline is lost due to expansion as the car warms up? Assume the coefficient of volume expansion of gasoline to be .0012/°C.\nIs the correct answer \".94 gal\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"no","correct":false,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer is about 0.93 gal, not 0.94 gal.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The volume expansion formula ΔV = βV₀ΔT with β = 0.0012/°C and V₀ = 20 gal is the right model.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Rounding ΔT up to 39 °C gives about 0.936 gal, which rounds to 0.94.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The temperature change of 70 F° equals about 38.9 C°, not 39 C°.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"With the exact ΔT of 38.9 °C, ΔV is about 0.933 gal, which rounds to 0.93 gal.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Ignoring the tank's own expansion is the intended approach, and it does not change the result.","confidence":0.8,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The answer 0.94 gal only follows from rounding ΔT to 39 °C, so it is slightly off.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The difference between 0.93 and 0.94 is small (under 1%), but the stated answer does not match the correct calculation.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":-0.8,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"